From: Waltteri Hooli <wahooli@localhost>
Subject: [PATCH] local: clk: qcom: tcsrcc-x1e80100: hand syscon our clockless regmap

The TCSR node is a clock provider and a syscon at once. pcie6a_phy's
qcom,4ln-config-sel = <&tcsr 0x1a000 0> makes phy-qcom-qmp-pcie look it up with
syscon_regmap_lookup_by_phandle_args(), and of_syscon_register() then does what
it does for any syscon: of_clk_get(np, 0) and regmap_mmio_attach_clk(), which is
a clk_prepare() held for as long as the regmap exists. The node's first clock is
RPMH_CXO_CLK -- the parent of the tcsr_*_clkref_en gates, not a bus clock -- and
clk-rpmh casts its vote on prepare. So the APSS asks AOP to keep the crystal on
in the active, wake and sleep sets alike, and XO shutdown, i.e. AOSS deep sleep,
can never happen.

Seen on the Lenovo Yoga Slim 7x (X1E-80-100): with every other XO consumer
traced off inside an s2idle, bi_tcxo reads enable 8 / prepare 9 awake, the odd
handle is "clock-controller@1fc0000, no connection id" (an of_clk_get() on the
tcsr node), bi_tcxo is disabled but never unprepared in the suspend path, and
xo.lvl is never written. Dropping the scm node's qcom,dload-mode removed one
creator of that syscon; this PHY lookup is the other.

of_syscon_register_regmap() exists for exactly this: a driver that owns the block
registers its regmap so later syscon lookups share it instead of creating a new
one. This driver already maps the TCSR without a clock, and the PHYs are
consumers of its clocks, so fw_devlink guarantees it probes before them.
Register the regmap before the clocks. -EOPNOTSUPP (no MFD_SYSCON) is silent;
anything else, -EEXIST above all, is worth a warning because it means a clocked
syscon already exists and the XO is pinned again.

--- a/drivers/clk/qcom/tcsrcc-x1e80100.c
+++ b/drivers/clk/qcom/tcsrcc-x1e80100.c
@@ -5,6 +5,7 @@
  */
 
 #include <linux/clk-provider.h>
+#include <linux/mfd/syscon.h>
 #include <linux/module.h>
 #include <linux/platform_device.h>
 #include <linux/regmap.h>
@@ -261,7 +262,30 @@
 
 static int tcsr_cc_x1e80100_probe(struct platform_device *pdev)
 {
-	return qcom_cc_probe(pdev, &tcsr_cc_x1e80100_desc);
+	struct regmap *regmap;
+	int ret;
+
+	regmap = qcom_cc_map(pdev, &tcsr_cc_x1e80100_desc);
+	if (IS_ERR(regmap))
+		return PTR_ERR(regmap);
+
+	/*
+	 * The node is also a syscon (pcie6a_phy's qcom,4ln-config-sel points
+	 * at it). Left to itself, of_syscon_register() takes the node's first
+	 * clock -- RPMH_CXO_CLK, the parent of the clkref gates, not a bus
+	 * clock -- and clk_prepare()s it for the life of the regmap. clk-rpmh
+	 * votes on prepare, so the APSS then demands the XO on in every RPMh
+	 * set, including sleep, and AOSS deep sleep is impossible. Hand syscon
+	 * this regmap instead; the TCSR needs no clock to be accessed. The
+	 * PHYs consume our clocks, so fw_devlink probes us first.
+	 */
+	ret = of_syscon_register_regmap(pdev->dev.of_node, regmap);
+	if (ret && ret != -EOPNOTSUPP)
+		dev_warn(&pdev->dev,
+			 "syscon regmap not registered (%d): a clocked syscon may pin the XO\n",
+			 ret);
+
+	return qcom_cc_really_probe(&pdev->dev, &tcsr_cc_x1e80100_desc, regmap);
 }
 
 static struct platform_driver tcsr_cc_x1e80100_driver = {
